  "result": "Short answer: yes, labs often use RL to make models better at tool calling. The idea is that the model tries a task, a score says whether the tools were called correctly, and training nudges the model toward the higher-scoring attempts. PPO is one method for making those nudges small and stable.\n\nI'm not going to derive it here. The derivation needs policy gradients and probability ratios, and you don't have the ML background for it yet. It also isn't what your assistant needs. Training a model takes a lot of compute and data, which doesn't fit your sponsor's budget. Your mission also puts training out of scope.\n\nThe useful part of that idea is the scoring. Write 20 real student requests, such as \"Is the laser cutter free Thursday?\", and record which tool call each one should trigger. Then run your assistant on them and count the misses. Most misses trace back to unclear tool descriptions, and those are cheap to fix.\n\nWant a lesson on that, covering tool descriptions and a small test set? First, which tool-call mistake do you expect your assistant to make most often?",
